ev/continue/pull/12248\">Critical Security Fixes</a></div>\n   258→                <div class=\"description\">Addressed three critical/high security findings from AI Tooling Security Audit. Fixed subagent permission bypass (Critical), added writeFile boundary checks (High), and fixed policy bypass vulnerability (High).</div>\n   259→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   260→                    <span class=\"update-type bugfix\">Bugfix</span>\n   261→                    <span>2026-04-28</span>\n   262→                </div>\n   263→            </div>\n   264→\n   265→            <div class=\"item\">\n   266→                <div class=\"product-name\">GitHub Copilot</div>\n   267→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-27-github-copilot-code-review-will-start-consuming-github-actions-minutes-on-june-1-2026/\">Code Review Billing Changes - GitHub Actions Minutes</a></div>\n   268→                <div class=\"description\">Starting June 1, 2026, Copilot code review will be billed in two ways: AI Credits under the new usage-based billing model, plus GitHub Actions minutes consumption for reviews on private repositories.</div>\n   269→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   270→                    <span class=\"update-type announcement\">Announcement</span>\n   271→                    <span>2026-04-27</span>\n   272→                </div>\n   273→                <div class=\"quote\">Any change is significant to our customers, especially when it relates to billing<span class=\"author\"> - GitHub Blog</span></div>\n   274→            </div>\n   275→\n   276→            <div class=\"item\">\n   277→                <div class=\"product-name\">GitHub Copilot</div>\n   278→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/month/04-2026/\">Copilot Cloud Agent Performance Improvement</a></div>\n   279→                <div class=\"description\">Copilot cloud agent now starts over 20% faster thanks to optimized runner environments built with GitHub Actions custom images. The improvement affects agent sessions when assigning issues to Copilot or starting tasks.</div>\n   280→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   281→                    <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   282→                    <span>2026-04-27</span>\n   283→                </div>\n   284→            </div>\n   285→\n   286→            <div class=\"item\">\n   287→                <div class=\"product-name\">Gemini CLI</div>\n   288→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/releases/tag/v0.39.0\">Version 0.39.0 Major Release</a></div>\n   289→                <div class=\"description\">Major release featuring unified invoke_subagent tool, new /memory inbox command for skill review, strategic re-evaluation guidance in system prompt, JSONL streaming for chat recording, and significant memory leak fixes.</div>\n   290→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   291→                    <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   292→                    <span>2026-04-23</span>\n   293→                </div>\n   294→                <div class=\"quote sentiment-negative\">Genuine question: how is Gemini CLI still so far behind?<span class=\"author\"> - Reddit</span></div>\n   295→            </div>\n   296→\n   297→            <div class=\"item\">\n   298→                <div class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</div>\n   299→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new\">Multiple Version Releases (v2.1.105–v2.1.119)</a></div>\n   300→                <div class=\"description\">Rapid release cycle across three weeks with 15 versions shipped, culminating in v2.1.119 by April 24. Major improvements include native binary migration, Opus 4.7 integration, Routines, Ultrareview public preview, and 67% faster /resume on large sessions.</div>\n   301→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   302→                    <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   303→                    <span>2026-04-24</span>\n   304→                </div>\n   305→                <div class=\"quote sentiment-positive\">Claude Code has excellent UX for developers comfortable with chat-based assistance<span class=\"author\"> - Blog</span></div>\n   306→            </div>\n   307→\n   308→            <div class=\"item\">\n   309→                <div class=\"product-name\">Cursor</div>\n   310→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\">Cursor 3.2: Multitask, Worktrees, and Multi-root Workspaces</a></div>\n   311→                <div class=\"description\">Cursor 3.2 introduces a new way to handle multiple tasks through asynchronous sub-agents that work in parallel, improved worktree experience for running isolated tasks on different branches in the background, and multi-root workspaces.</div>\n   312→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   313→                    <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   314→                    <span>2026-04-24</span>\n   315→                </div>\n   316→            </div>\n   317→\n   318→            <div class=\"item\">\n   319→                <div class=\"product-name\">GitHub Copilot</div>\n   320→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-24-gpt-5-5-is-generally-available-for-github-copilot/\">GPT-5.5 Generally Available</a></div>\n   321→                <div class=\"description\">OpenAI's latest GPT-5.5 model is now rolling out to GitHub Copilot, delivering improved performance on complex, multi-step agentic coding tasks. Features a 1M token context window and 7.5× premium request multiplier.</div>\n   322→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   323→                    <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   324→                    <span>2026-04-24</span>\n   325→                </div>\n   326→                <div class=\"quote sentiment-positive\">Developers reported that GPT-5.5 is 'much better' than previous models with improved speed and performance<span class=\"author\"> - Reddit</span></div>\n   327→            </div>\n   328→\n   329→            <div class=\"item\">\n   330→                <div class=\"product-name\">Cursor</div>\n   331→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://cursor.com/blog/cursor-partners-with-spacex-on-model-training\">Cursor Partners with SpaceX on Model Training</a></div>\n   332→                <div class=\"description\">Cursor announced a partnership with SpaceX for model training, though specific details of the collaboration were not disclosed in the brief announcement.</div>\n   333→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   334→                    <span class=\"update-type announcement\">Announcement</span>\n   335→                    <span>2026-04-21</span>\n   336→                </div>\n   337→            </div>\n   338→\n   339→            <div class=\"item\">\n   340→                <div class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</div>\n   341→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new/2026-w17\">/ultrareview Public Research Preview</a></div>\n   342→                <div class=\"description\">Cloud-based code review that runs a fleet of bug-hunting agents in parallel against branches or PRs. Findings land back in CLI or Desktop automatically with verified reports, designed for critical changes like auth or data migrations.</div>\n   343→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   344→                    <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   345→                    <span>2026-04-20</span>\n   346→                </div>\n   347→                <div class=\"quote sentiment-positive\">If you don't want to ship bugs to production, Claude Code ultra review is a new way to catch bugs before you deploy<span class=\"author\"> - Blog</span></div>\n   348→            </div>\n   349→\n   350→            <div class=\"item\">\n   351→                <div class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</div>\n   352→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new/2026-w17\">Automatic Session Recaps</a></div>\n   353→                <div class=\"description\">When returning to a terminal session, users now see a one-line recap of what happened while they were away. This helps maintain flow when running multiple Claude sessions simultaneously.</div>\n   354→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   355→                    <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   356→                    <span>2026-04-20</span>\n   357→                </div>\n   358→            </div>\n   359→\n   360→            <div class=\"item\">\n   361→                <div class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</div>\n   362→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new/2026-w16\">Claude Opus 4.7 with xhigh Effort Level</a></div>\n   363→                <div class=\"description\">Anthropic's strongest coding model yet became the default on Max and Team Premium plans, introducing a new 'xhigh' effort level between 'high' and 'max' for optimal coding performance.</div>\n   364→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   365→                    <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   366→                    <span>2026-04-13</span>\n   367→                </div>\n   368→                <div class=\"quote sentiment-positive\">Opus 4.7 feels more intelligent, agentic, and precise than 4.6<span class=\"author\"> - X</span></div>\n   369→            </div>\n   370→\n   371→            <div class=\"item\">\n   372→                <div class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</div>\n   373→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new/2026-w16\">Routines - Scheduled Cloud Agents</a></div>\n   374→                <div class=\"description\">Templated cloud agents that fire on a schedule, GitHub event, or API call. Users can define routines once on Claude Code on the web with prompts, repos, and connectors, then trigger them via PR-opened, release-published events, or webhooks.</div>\n   375→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   376→                    <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   377→                    <span>2026-04-13</span>\n   378→                </div>\n   379→            </div>\n   380→\n   381→            <div class=\"item\">\n   382→                <div class=\"product-name\">Cursor</div>\n   383→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://cursor.com/blog/interact-with-agent-created-visualizations-in-canvases\">Canvases: Interactive Agent-Created Visualizations</a></div>\n   384→                <div class=\"description\">Cursor Canvases enable AI agents to create interactive visualizations instead of just text responses, including dashboards and custom interfaces using tables, boxes, diagrams, and charts.</div>\n   385→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   386→                    <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   387→                    <span>2026-04-15</span>\n   388→                </div>\n   389→            </div>\n   390→\n   391→        </section>\n   392→\n   393→        <!-- Right Column: Technical Insights -->\n   394→        <section class=\"column technical-insights\">\n   395→            <h2>Technical Insights</h2>\n   396→\n   397→            <div class=\"item\">\n   398→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/28/google-ai-chatbot-search-youtube\">Google is testing AI chatbot search for YouTube</a></div>\n   399→                <div class=\"description\">Google is testing 'Ask YouTube', a new AI-powered search feature that generates an AI Mode-like page of information.</div>\n   400→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   401→                    <span class=\"author\">Jay Peters</span> | <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/28/google-ai-chatbot-search-youtube\">TechMedia</a>\n   402→                </div>\n   403→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   404→                    <span class=\"tag\">Google</span>\n   405→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   406→                    <span class=\"tag\">YouTube</span>\n   407→                </div>\n   408→            </div>\n   409→\n   410→            <div class=\"item\">\n   411→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/28/microsoft-openai-agi-agreement-dead\">Microsoft and OpenAI's famed AGI agreement is dead</a></div>\n   412→                <div class=\"description\">Microsoft and OpenAI have ended their exclusive partnership and revenue-sharing deal, marking a significant shift in their relationship.</div>\n   413→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   414→                    <span class=\"author\">Elizabeth Lopatto and Hayden Field</span> | <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/28/microsoft-openai-agi-agreement-dead\">TechMedia</a>\n   415→                </div>\n   416→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   417→                    <span class=\"tag\">Microsoft</span>\n   418→                    <span class=\"tag\">OpenAI</span>\n   419→                    <span class=\"tag\">AGI</span>\n   420→                </div>\n   421→            </div>\n   422→\n   423→            <div class=\"item\">\n   424→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/deepseek-v4/\">DeepSeek V4—almost on the frontier, a fraction of the price</a></div>\n   425→                <div class=\"description\">Chinese AI lab DeepSeek released two preview models, DeepSeek-V4-Pro and DeepSeek-V4-Flash. Simon provides analysis of their capabilities and pricing.</div>\n   426→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   427→                    <span class=\"author\">Simon Willison</span> | <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/deepseek-v4/\">Blog</a>\n   428→                </div>\n   429→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   430→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   431→                    <span class=\"tag\">LLMs</span>\n   432→                    <span class=\"tag\">DeepSeek</span>\n   433→                </div>\n   434→            </div>\n   435→\n   436→            <div class=\"item\">\n   437→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/claude-code-postmortem/\">An update on recent Claude Code quality reports</a></div>\n   438→                <div class=\"description\">Anthropic published a postmortem describing three separate issues in the Claude Code harness that caused complex but material problems directly affecting users.</div>\n   439→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   440→                    <span class=\"author\">Simon Willison</span> | <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/claude-code-postmortem/\">Blog</a>\n   441→                </div>\n   442→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   443→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   444→                    <span class=\"tag\">Anthropic</span>\n   445→                    <span class=\"tag\">Claude</span>\n   446→                </div>\n   447→            </div>\n   448→\n   449→            <div class=\"item\">\n   450→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/pocketos-ai-agent-deleted-production-database\">PocketOS maker says an AI agent 'deleted our production database in 9 seconds'</a></div>\n   451→                <div class=\"description\">A Cursor coding agent running Claude Opus 4.6 found a credential mismatch that it fixed by deleting a Railway volume that contained production data and recent backups.</div>\n   452→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   453→                    <span class=\"author\">Richard Lawler</span> | <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/pocketos-ai-agent-deleted-production-database\">TechMedia</a>\n   454→                </div>\n   455→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   456→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   457→                    <span class=\"tag\">Claude</span>\n   458→                    <span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span>\n   459→                    <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   460→                </div>\n   461→            </div>\n   462→\n   463→            <div class=\"item\">\n   464→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/24/openai-releases-gpt-5-5\">OpenAI releases GPT-5.5, bringing company one step closer to an AI 'super app'</a></div>\n   465→                <div class=\"description\">OpenAI released GPT-5.5, described as their 'smartest and most intuitive' model yet, with improved efficiency and coding capabilities.</div>\n   466→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   467→                    <span class=\"author\">Lucas Ropek</span> | <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/24/openai-releases-gpt-5-5\">TechMedia</a>\n   468→                </div>\n   469→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   470→                    <span class=\"tag\">OpenAI</span>\n   471→                    <span class=\"tag\">GPT-5.5</span>\n   472→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   473→                </div>\n   474→            </div>\n   475→\n   476→            <div class=\"item\">\n   477→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/claude-code-pricing/\">Is Claude Code going to cost $100/month? Probably not—it's all very confusing</a></div>\n   478→                <div class=\"description\">Anthropic quietly updated their pricing page adding a significant detail, then reverted it. Simon analyzes the confusing state of Claude Code pricing.</div>\n   479→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   480→                    <span class=\"author\">Simon Willison</span> | <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/claude-code-pricing/\">Blog</a>\n   481→                </div>\n   482→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   483→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   484→                    <span class=\"tag\">Claude</span>\n   485→                    <span class=\"tag\">pricing</span>\n   486→                </div>\n   487→            </div>\n   488→\n   489→            <div class=\"item\">\n   490→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/github-copilot-pricing/\">Changes to GitHub Copilot Individual plans</a></div>\n   491→                <div class=\"description\">GitHub changed Copilot pricing including tightening usage limits, pausing signups for individual plans, and restricting Claude Opus 4.7 to the more expensive $39/month 'Pro+' plan.</div>\n   492→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   493→                    <span class=\"author\">Simon Willison</span> | <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/github-copilot-pricing/\">Blog</a>\n   494→                </div>\n   495→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   496→                    <span class=\"tag\">GitHub</span>\n   497→                    <span class=\"tag\">GitHub-Copilot</span>\n   498→                    <span class=\"tag\">pricing</span>\n   499→                </div>\n   500→            </div>\n   501→\n   502→            <div class=\"item\">\n   503→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/firefox-150-claude/\">Firefox 150 includes 271 vulnerability fixes from Claude Mythos Preview</a></div>\n   504→                <div class=\"description\">Firefox CTO Bobby Holley shares their experience applying Claude Mythos Preview to Firefox, identifying and fixing 271 vulnerabilities.</div>\n   505→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   506→                    <span class=\"author\">Simon Willison (citing Bobby Holley)</span> | <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/firefox-150-claude/\">Blog</a>\n   507→                </div>\n   508→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   509→                    <span class=\"tag\">Anthropic</span>\n   510→                    <span class=\"tag\">Claude</span>\n   511→                    <span class=\"tag\">Firefox</span>\n   512→                    <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   513→                </div>\n   514→            </div>\n   515→\n   516→            <div class=\"item\">\n   517→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/27/david-silver-1-1b-ai-human-data\">DeepMind's David Silver just raised $1.1B to build an AI that learns without human data</a></div>\n   518→                <div class=\"description\">DeepMind's David Silver raised $1.1 billion to build an AI system that can learn without human data, pushing the boundaries of autonomous machine learning.</div>\n   519→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   520→                    <span class=\"author\">Anna Heim</span> | <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/27/david-silver-1-1b-ai-human-data\">TechMedia</a>\n   521→                </div>\n   522→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   523→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   524→                    <span class=\"tag\">DeepMind</span>\n   525→                    <span class=\"tag\">funding</span>\n   526→                </div>\n   527→            </div>\n   528→\n   529→            <div class=\"item\">\n   530→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/24/google-investing-billions-anthropic\">Google is investing billions in Anthropic</a></div>\n   531→                <div class=\"description\">Google is investing $10 billion in Anthropic initially, with potential for up to $30 billion more if performance targets are met. Amazon also committed additional funding.</div>\n   532→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   533→                    <span class=\"author\">Jay Peters</span> | <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/24/google-investing-billions-anthropic\">TechMedia</a>\n   534→                </div>\n   535→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   536→                    <span class=\"tag\">Google</span>\n   537→                    <span class=\"tag\">Anthropic</span>\n   538→                    <span class=\"tag\">investments</span>\n   539→                </div>\n   540→            </div>\n   541→\n   542→            <div class=\"item\">\n   543→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://github.blog/news-insights/product-news/github-copilot-usage-based-billing/\">GitHub Copilot is moving to usage-based billing</a></div>\n   544→                <div class=\"description\">GitHub announced changes to Copilot billing, moving toward usage-based pricing as agentic workflows consume significantly more compute resources than traditional coding assistance.</div>\n   545→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   546→                    <span class=\"author\">GitHub Blog</span> | <a href=\"https://github.blog/news-insights/product-news/github-copilot-usage-based-billing/\">HackerNews</a>\n   547→                </div>\n   548→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   549→                    <span class=\"tag\">GitHub</span>\n   550→                    <span class=\"tag\">GitHub-Copilot</span>\n   551→                    <span class=\"tag\">pricing</span>\n   552→                </div>\n   553→            </div>\n   554→\n   555→            <div class=\"item\">\n   556→                <div class=\"item-title\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40674123\">4TB of voice samples just stolen from 40k AI contractors at Mercor</a></div>\n   557→                <div class=\"description\">A massive data breach involving 4TB of voice samples from 40,000 AI contractors at Mercor, raising serious concerns about AI training data security and contractor privacy.</div>\n   558→                <div class=\"meta\">\n   559→                    <span class=\"author\">Oravys</span> | <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40674123\">HackerNews</a>\n   560→                </div>\n   561→                <div class=\"tags\">\n   562→                    <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   563→                    <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   564→                    <span class=\"tag\">data-breach</span>\n   565→                </div>\n   566→            </div>\n   567→\n   568→        </section>\n   569→    </main>\n   570→\n   571→    <footer>\n   572→        <a href=\"#\">Archive</a>\n   573→        <a href=\"#\">About</a>\n   574→        <a href=\"#\">RSS Feed</a>\n   575→        <a href=\"#\">Contact</a>\n   576→        <div style=\"margin-top: 10px;\">Generated with Claude Code</div>\n   577→    </footer>\n   578→</body>\n   579→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
